## How Docker Layer Caching Works
Docker builds images **layer by layer** and caches each one. If a layer hasn't changed, Docker reuses the cache instead of rebuilding it — saving you time.

The rule is:
> **If a layer changes, every layer below it gets rebuilt**

---

## Why RUN Before ENV Matters

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

# ✅ Correct order
RUN apk add --no-cache curl bash git    # changes rarely
ENV STUDENT_NAME="John"                 # changes often
CMD sh -c "echo ${STUDENT_NAME}"
```

vs

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

# ❌ Your teacher warned against this
ENV STUDENT_NAME="John"                 # changes often
RUN apk add --no-cache curl bash git    # forces reinstall every time ENV changes
CMD sh -c "echo ${STUDENT_NAME}"
```

In the second example, every time you update `STUDENT_NAME`, Docker invalidates the cache and **reinstalls all your packages from scratch** unnecessarily.

---

## The General Rule of Thumb
Arrange layers from **least changing → most changing**:

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest          # 1. base image (never changes)

RUN apk add --no-cache \    # 2. dependencies (rarely changes)
    curl \
    bash

COPY . /app                 # 3. project files (sometimes changes)

ENV STUDENT_NAME="John"     # 4. config/variables (changes often)

CMD sh -c "echo hello"      # 5. startup command (changes most)
```

--

it's about **building smart and saving time** during rebuilds. 👍
